 "brief": "PROPERTIES OF CLOSED 3-BRAIDS — We show that 3-braid links with given (non-zero) Alexander o r Jones polynomial are ﬁnitely many, and can be effectively determined.",
 "extended": "**PROPERTIES OF CLOSED 3-BRAIDS**\n\n*AND OTHER LINK BRAID REPRESENTATIONS*\n\n68 pp, 69 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** We show that 3-braid links with given (non-zero) Alexander o r Jones polynomial are ﬁnitely many, and can be effectively determined. We classify among closed 3-b raids strongly quasi-positive and ﬁbered ones, and show that 3-braid links have a unique incompressible Seifert surface. We also classify the positive braid words with Morton-Williams-Franks bound 3 and show that closed positive braids of braid index 3 are closed positive 3-braids. For closed braids on more strings, we study the alternating l inks occurring. In particular we classify those of braid index 4, and show that their Morton-Williams-Franks inequality is exact. Finally, we use the Burau representation to obtain new braid index criteria, including an efﬁcient 4-braid test.\n\n**Outline.** 1 Introduction 2; 2 Preliminaries, basic deﬁnitions and conventi; 3 Xu’s form and Seifert surfaces 11; 4 Polynomial invariants 15; 5 Positivity of 3-braid links 26; 6 Studying alternating links by braid index 38; 7 Applications of the representation theory 42; 2 Preliminaries, basic deﬁnitions and conventi; 2.1 Links and link diagrams; 2.2 Polynomial link invariants; 2.3 Semiadequacy 7; 2.4 Braids and braid words; 2.5 Braid representations of links 9; 2.6 Gauß sum invariants; 3 Xu’s form and Seifert surfaces; 3.1 Strongly quasi-positive links among links ; 3.2 Uniqueness of minimal genus Seifert surfac; 1 (σ−1\n\n**Extracted claims (proposals, not verified).**\n- Theorem 1.1: If L is a strongly quasi-positive link, and has braid index 3, the n L is has a strongly quasi-positive\n- Theorem 1.2: Any 3-braid link has a unique (non-closed) incompressible surface.\n- Theorem 1.3: If L is a positive braid link, and has braid index 3, then L is a positive 3-braid link.\n- Theorem 2.1: Assume that D is a connected A-adequate diagram. Then V0V1 = χ(G(A)) − 1 and\n- Theorem 1.1: follows relatively easily from the work in [St2], but it is a good starting point for the later more substantial\n- Theorem 3.1: Every 3-braid link has a weakly unique minimal genus Seifert surface.",
